A||N||12||Formula88||Cold Air Intake - an alternative||||6||
Z||000000||Formula88||04-30-2001||03:49 PM||||Well, I just did my own cold air intake for my Formula and thought I'd share.  Based on the article I read from Herb Adams about the factory air intake not causing any HP loss (they notice no change on the dyno with the air intake completely removed) I was mainly interested in a better induction sound, and to make sure the car gets plenty of air.  All I did was remove the water diverter box from the fender well.  Then I got some flexible air intake tubing from Pep Boys (I'll post the exact size when I can) and ran it from the bottom of the factory air cleaner housing.  I then connected the other end of the hose to a 4" section of 3" ID PVC pipe.  The rubber fitting in the fender easily stretched over the PCV for a nice snug fit, and then bolted right back onto the fender.  So now I have a mean induction roar, better airflow, and I don't have to take my fender liner off to change the air cleaner!  Not to mention this system is sealed all the way to the fender intake, and doesn't just hang an open element filter in the wheel well.  You can definately feel the difference in the intake just by putting your hand up to the fender vent.  Total cost, about $20.00  <P>I used Mighty Flow brand air intake hose.  Part number 96086.  It's 3-19/32" DIA x 32-1/2" Long. I had to use some duct tape around the air cleaner base to build up the diameter slightly so the hose would fit snug, but on the other end it fits perfectly. You could probably get just add a hose clamp on the air cleaner base, but since it has a non-adjustable clamp built in, I decided to use it for the quick release feature.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||Alex4mula||04-30-2001||04:00 PM||||Sorry but I don't understand where is your filter located. Could you explain? Is it between the TBS and PCV pipe? Or between PCV pipe and original intake base? Tks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||04-30-2001||04:22 PM||||The air filter is factory.  It wasn't changed.  All I did was remove the piping from the air filter housing out to the fender vent and replaced all that with flexible tubing.  All this does is eliminate the water separator (which also acts as an intake noise silencer).  The sound alone is worth the effort.  I chose to keep the factory air filter since you don't get much, if any improvement by going to an open element unit, and I didn't want to have to change my filter through the fender.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||04-30-2001||05:09 PM||||Oooh.. Ok. Now I understand. Thanks for the explanation. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-30-2001||05:25 PM||||I knocked the water seperator (air choker) out of my intake with a screwdriver.  One question I haven't seen answered is what are the chances, if any, of water getting sucked into the filter with it removed?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||05:59 PM||||oops<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||05:59 PM||||I sorta did the same thing. just used stock air cleaner housing with a K&N filter, but I left the box with none of the baffles since I drive year round including rain. Then used flex tubing to connect the box and filter housing. At the box intake I used more flex tube to connect to the back of the side scoop on my 'Ferrari' kit body. So I also have ram air.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||04-30-2001||06:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR>One question I haven't seen answered is what are the chances, if any, of water getting sucked into the filter with it removed?<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Minimal. <P>1. Unless it is raining like C&D, there isn't going to be much water around the intake.<P>B) For the water to be sucked up from the intake to the filter, you'd have to have a pressure differential of about 1 in Hg from the filter to the outside pressure.<P>On the gripping hand) A little water isn't going to affect your filter/intake that much. Some hot rodders used to inject water into the intake for better peformance.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||04-30-2001||07:52 PM||||If it was raining hard enough to get water in the intake tube, then that's not what you'll have to worry about.  I noticed after some hard rains this spring that water will stand on the top of the air cleaner lid and seep in around the bolt hole.  My filter got wet from this water with the car not running, so that'll be more likely than any getting in through the fender.  If the water coming in the top isn't a problem, I don't see how any that might make it up through the intake vent could matter.  Besides, the filter will stop it.  Worst case scenario, you get a soggy air filter.  ||||reg||6
Z||000009||banditbalz||04-30-2001||10:22 PM||||I did the same cold air setup 9 years ago and it hasn't let me down yet. If you happen to suck any water in, it would be a benefit to the engine as I see it. Anyways, the removal of the water seperator has cut my quarter mile times by 1/10 of a sec. on average. Addition of the holley scoop cut another 1/10th. Overall, the setup has given my GT a nice growl without the embarassing "Hoover vacuum" sound of a true cold air induction.<P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||14
Z||000010||fyerstarter||04-30-2001||10:29 PM||||I did the same mod and had it about a year before I did the true cold air intake. I noticed little if any performance increase, but the sound did get a little better. However since I went to a real cold air intake It's been a much more noticeable difference. I love the "hoover vacuum sound"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But anything is better than stock, least that's my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||10:43 PM||||prob not much of a water prob with stock grill. but if you put a scoop of anykind it will. If its raining I have a 6 inch round hole facing directly forward, and it gets enough water to fill a bucket in minutes, luckily with the box (case only) still there 95% if it runs out the bottom of it instead of going in my air filter.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||05-01-2001||02:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fyerstarter:<BR><B>I did the same mod and had it about a year before I did the true cold air intake. I noticed little if any performance increase, but the sound did get a little better. However since I went to a real cold air intake It's been a much more noticeable difference. I love the "hoover vacuum sound"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But anything is better than stock, least that's my opinion.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What's the difference between my system that runs from the fender vent to the engine (or the stock setup for that matter) and a "true cold air intake?"  If you've got an open element air filter in the fender well or the engine comparment, it's less of a "true" cold air intake than factory.  It may be a higher flowing and higher performance setup, but I'm not sure what you mean by true.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||5
